/* Typechecking only. This config emits nothing.

   `shared/` is TypeScript that its consumers compile themselves:
   Next.js compiles it through `transpilePackages` in
   next/next.config.ts, and wrangler's esbuild compiles it when it
   bundles worker.js. Neither reads a .js file from this directory,
   and there is no build step here that writes one.

   That is deliberate and it replaced the opposite arrangement.
   Compiling these modules to committed .js beside their own source
   meant three files where there is now one, a build script, and a
   check to notice when somebody edited the output instead of the
   input. Every one of those exists to serve a compile step that
   neither runtime needs.

   What this file is for is `npx tsc -p shared/tsconfig.json`,
   which answers "does shared/ typecheck on its own" without
   waiting on either consumer's build. `noEmit` is what keeps it
   an answer rather than an artefact.

   `lib` carries DOM because headers.ts constructs a `Response`.
   Both runtimes have one; neither is a browser.

   `allowImportingTsExtensions` because that is how the imports in
   here are written, `./curricula/money.ts`, and they have to be:
   node reads these files with no build step and resolves the real
   filename. The two configs that COMPILE content.ts and the
   curricula to `aab/` pair it with
   `rewriteRelativeImportExtensions`, which is what turns the
   specifier back into a `.js` a browser can fetch. */
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM"],
    "strict": true,
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true
  },
  "include": ["*.ts", "curricula/*.ts"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules"]
}
